Problem Statement
Livestock farming is essential for food security and rural income, 
but small and medium-scale farms often struggle with low productivity.
Most farmers do not track data systematically; instead, animal lineage, 
health history, breeding cycles, and feed records are kept in manual logs or not recorded at all.
This makes it hard to find the root causes of low yields, such as poor-performing breeds or high feed costs. 
Without a central system, managers cannot see real-time drops in milk or meat production, 
making it difficult to make profitable adjustments or plan for the future.

Proposed Solution

This project builds a central Oracle database with PL/SQL to track and analyze livestock lifecycles and farm yields. 
The system will store profiles for each animal, including family history, medical treatments, feed use, and daily production outputs. 
By using built-in database rules, the system ensures data remains accurate and error-free. 
It will also use automated PL/SQL programs to calculate farm performance metrics, send alerts, and 
create clean reports for managers, turning raw data into clear steps to improve breeding and feed efficiency.


Data Source
The system will use an open-source dataset from Kaggle containing real agricultural records for over 500 animals. 
It includes details like animal IDs, breeds, birth dates, feed intake, health records, and daily milk or meat weights. 
All personal information and specific farm names have been removed to protect privacy and follow educational rules.


Planned Tables

FARM_UNITS: Tracks the different barns or sections of the farm.
 LIVESTOCK_REGISTRY: Stores animal profiles, including Tag ID, breed, birth date, gender, and parents' IDs.
 PRODUCTION_LOG: Records daily milk or meat collection times and weights.
 HEALTH_INTERVENTIONS: Tracks medical history, treatments, drug dosages, and the treating veterinarian.
 BREEDING_LOG: Logs insemination dates, sire details, pregnancy check dates, and expected birth dates.
 FEED_CONSUMPTION: Monitors daily feed types, costs, and amounts given to each section or group.

Planned PL/SQL Features

Stored Procedure (LOG_DAILY_YIELD): Takes an animal's Tag ID and yield weight to safely save 
the daily records into the PRODUCTION_LOG.
 Stored Function (GET_FEED_EFFICIENCY): Compares an animal's feed intake against
its production output to calculate its Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR).
 Cursor Implementation (GENERATE_HEALTH_REPORT): Loops through the health table inside a management package to 
list animals currently under medication restrictions, keeping unsafe products out of delivery.

 Database Trigger (VALIDATE_BREEDING_AGE): Checks the animal's birth date before allowing a new entry in the BREEDING_LOG 
to prevent breeding animals that are too young.
 Exception Handling: Standard error catchers in every program to block duplicate entries
or negative yield numbers and save errors to an audit log.


Innovation

To make the database easy to use without writing SQL queries, 
a live Power BI Analytics Dashboard will connect directly to the Oracle database. 
This dashboard will display key metrics through clear charts, showing daily yield trends, 
comparing which breeds produce the most, and tracking feed costs against income. 
This gives managers a fast, visual way to see their best animals and make business decisions


